    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "During development, brain connectivity is modified by changes in experience and neural activity. These plastic modifications in the structure and function of synapses, the connections between neurons, are known as synaptic plasticity. Synaptic plasticity can alter the way neural circuits compute and ultimately lead to changes in behavior. In the visual system, vision is required for the refinement of connections in primary visual cortex (V1), especially during a developmental critical period. However, the molecular mechanisms by which changes in experience are translated into synapse modifications are unclear. This study describes a potential role for Qa-1, a mouse major histocompatibility complex protein, in visual cortical plasticity during the postnatal critical period. The role of Qa-1 in the immune system has been investigated, but we show that it may have a separate function in the brain that depends on neural activity. Multiplex in situ hybridization experiments showed that Qa-1 mRNA is specifically expressed in deep layer cortical excitatory neurons. This expression begins during the visual cortical critical period, correlating with a period of increased plasticity. Furthermore, Qa-1 mRNA in V1 is regulated by changes in visual experience. Exposure to darkness during the critical period resulted in lower expression of Qa-1 in mouse V1, and re-exposure to light restored Qa-1 expression to control levels. These results support a role for Qa-1 in transducing changes in neural activity into molecular signals for synaptic plasticity. Our results indicate that Qa-1 may be involved in activity-dependent plasticity and help clarify the molecular mechanisms by which synapses change in response to experience during development. Uncovering the molecular basis of synaptic plasticity in development will lead to a better understanding of neural developmental disorders and neurodegenerative disease.",
